      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Congress answered the call of the judiciary by enacting section 1658, a bright line rule that provides a default statute of limitations of 4 years for any civil action arising under an act of Congress after December 1st, 1990."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice",
      "case_id": "2003-02-1205",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "912d27895b44e23898e13fb2ccd5b49aa818789204cccbcc9be333d26df2c0ac",
      "evidence_id": "ev-912d27895b44-0000-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: Ms. Gorman, one of the anomalies that if -- if you are complaining about a refusal to hire, a discriminatory refusal to hire, the limitation would be 2 years, but if you're complaining about a discriminatory firing, it would be 4 years."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States",
      "case_id": "2003-02-1205",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "912d27895b44e23898e13fb2ccd5b49aa818789204cccbcc9be333d26df2c0ac",
      "evidence_id": "ev-912d27895b44-0000-0006",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 17,
        "start": 17
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Petitioners' claims for racial discrimination in the terms and condition of employment are subject to the uniform statute of limitations set forth in 28 U.S.C. 1658 because those claims were created by and therefore arise under the Civil Rights Act of 1991, an act of Congress enacted after December 1 -\u00ad"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States",
      "case_id": "2003-02-1205",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "912d27895b44e23898e13fb2ccd5b49aa818789204cccbcc9be333d26df2c0ac",
      "evidence_id": "ev-912d27895b44-0000-0007",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 18,
        "start": 18
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the compromise that was adopted was that the uniform statute of limitations would apply on a going-forward basis with respect to causes of actions that were created by acts of Congress enacted after December 1990."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2003-02-1205",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "912d27895b44e23898e13fb2ccd5b49aa818789204cccbcc9be333d26df2c0ac",
      "evidence_id": "ev-912d27895b44-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 19,
        "start": 19
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: So you're saying we -- we will have to resolve these -- these circuit splits for no purpose whatever except to decide whether the statute of limitations applies. Right? We'll have to -"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Respondent",
      "case_id": "2003-02-1205",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "912d27895b44e23898e13fb2ccd5b49aa818789204cccbcc9be333d26df2c0ac",
      "evidence_id": "ev-912d27895b44-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. GARRE: We don't think it applies to State causes of action at all."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Respondent",
      "case_id": "2003-02-1205",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "912d27895b44e23898e13fb2ccd5b49aa818789204cccbcc9be333d26df2c0ac",
      "evidence_id": "ev-912d27895b44-0001-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "And we think that that interpretation requires the conclusion in this case that petitioners' claims are governed by the statute of limitations in section 1658."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice",
      "case_id": "2003-02-1205",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "912d27895b44e23898e13fb2ccd5b49aa818789204cccbcc9be333d26df2c0ac",
      "evidence_id": "ev-912d27895b44-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 36,
        "start": 36
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: And that's suggesting that -\u00ad"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Phillips",
      "case_id": "2003-02-1205",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "912d27895b44e23898e13fb2ccd5b49aa818789204cccbcc9be333d26df2c0ac",
      "evidence_id": "ev-912d27895b44-0002-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. PHILLIPS: Is it irrational? Yes. Is it unconstitutional? Probably not under the standards that loosely govern these kinds of issues unless somebody can attach it to some kind of -\u00ad"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Kevin C. Newsom",
      "case_id": "2003-02-1205",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "912d27895b44e23898e13fb2ccd5b49aa818789204cccbcc9be333d26df2c0ac",
      "evidence_id": "ev-912d27895b44-0002-0001",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
